 "properties": {
  "project": "sf-waterfront-flood-defense",
  "source": "Port of San Francisco, USACE Flood Study Update and Next Steps (Port Commission, Aug 11, 2026), slide 13, Draft Phasing and Implementation Strategy, georeferenced to DataSF street centrelines",
  "sourceLabel": "Port Commission presentation (Aug 11, 2026), phasing map",
  "sourceUrl": "https://www.sfport.com/media/11441/download",
  "accuracy": "approximate",
  "accuracyNote": "Approximate alignment: the shoreline stretches the Port's phasing map marks as flood-impact segments, which the Recommended Plan defends ('indicates approximately where to build flood defenses', Recommended Plan Highlights, p. 18). 11.3 miles as drawn, because the line follows the water's edge into Mission Creek and Islais Creek (both banks) and around the Pier 80-96 terminals; the Port describes its jurisdiction as '7.5 miles' of shoreline. The map is small-scale (about 7 m per pixel) and fitted to DataSF streets with a trimmed RMS of 2.58 m, so the line is good to roughly 20-40 m. Where each defense sits (bayward, at the shoreline or inland) is decided in design. Reaches split at the map's dashed boundaries. The site shown is a 60 m corridor around the alignment, for selecting it on the map; it is not a property line.",
  "license": "Traced facts from a public Port of San Francisco presentation (the PDF is not redistributed). Georeference: DataSF Streets - Active and Retired (3psu-pn9h), Open Data Commons Public Domain Dedication and License (PDDL) 1.0; City and County of San Francisco. Check: DataSF SF Shoreline and Islands (txuc-3kzm), ODC PDDL 1.0.",
